<!-- ==========================================
     IOP WEEK 04
     BREAK UP THE BFFLs
     ========================================== -->

<article class="iop-essay-entry">

  <p class="iop-essay-label">
    WEEK 04 / COGNITIVE DISTORTIONS
  </p>

  <h3>
    BREAK UP THE BFFLs:<br>
    THE BRAIN AND COGNITIVE DISTORTIONS
  </h3>

  <div class="iop-essay-body">

    <p>
      <em>Hell, nobody will even notice I'm gone</em>, I wrote in my jail
      journal. It was winter of 2024, and I had only been incarcerated for
      a month or two. I wasn't receiving mail or many text messages, and I
      was convinced that the world outside had already forgotten me. My
      friends, family, and neighbors were living their lives and had erased
      me from their memories. Was that really the case, though, or was my
      brain playing tricks on me?
    </p>

    <p>
      I was experiencing automatic thoughts—thoughts that happened so quickly
      I didn't notice them, even though they were affecting my mood. They
      weren't an accurate picture of reality, and their best friends,
      cognitive distortions, were convincing me that my beliefs about the
      world and myself were facts.
    </p>

    <p>
      Cognitive distortions are patterns of thinking that can shape how we
      interpret situations, feel, and act. I wasn't examining my internal
      narrative, which might have helped me recognize the connections between
      my thoughts, feelings, and behavior. Managing stress in jail was like
      trying to shove my foot into the wrong shoe size. Still, learning to
      notice what my brain was saying could have helped me respond more
      deliberately. I wish I had known that then, but I was reeling and just
      trying to survive.
    </p>

    <p>
      Cognitive distortions and the brain are tighter than Cheech and Chong.
      They show up a lot. Here’s a comprehensive list:
    </p>

    <!-- COGNITIVE DISTORTIONS -->
    <ul class="iop-essay-list">

      <li>
        <strong>Absolute Thinking:</strong>
        Using extreme words like <em>always, never, everyone,</em>
        and <em>nobody</em>.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>All-or-Nothing Thinking:</strong>
        Seeing things in extremes, with no middle ground.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Blaming:</strong>
        Placing excessive responsibility for your problems or feelings
        on others.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Catastrophizing:</strong>
        Assuming the worst possible outcome will happen.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Discounting the Positive:</strong>
        Dismissing achievements or positive experiences.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Emotional Reasoning:</strong>
        Believing something is true simply because it feels true.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Fortune Telling:</strong>
        Predicting negative outcomes without enough evidence.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Jumping to Conclusions:</strong>
        Making assumptions without enough information.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Labeling:</strong>
        Defining yourself or someone else by one mistake or characteristic.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Magnification:</strong>
        Exaggerating the importance of mistakes, flaws, or problems.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Mental Filtering:</strong>
        Focusing on negative details while ignoring positive ones.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Mind Reading:</strong>
        Assuming you know what other people are thinking.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Minimization:</strong>
        Downplaying accomplishments, strengths, or problems.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Overgeneralization:</strong>
        Assuming one experience represents a recurring pattern.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Personalization:</strong>
        Taking responsibility for things outside your control.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Perfectionism:</strong>
        Setting unrealistic standards and treating anything less than
        perfection as failure.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Should/Must Statements:</strong>
        Imposing rigid expectations on yourself or others.
      </li>

      <li>
        <strong>Unfair Comparisons:</strong>
        Comparing your weaknesses to other people's strengths.
      </li>

    </ul>

    <p>
      Thoughts, emotions, and behaviors all influence one another in what is
      known as the cognitive triangle. The image below shows that relationship.
    </p>

    <%= image_tag "cognitive_triangle.jpg",
        alt: "Cognitive triangle showing thoughts, emotions, and behaviors",
        class: "iop-essay-image",
        loading: "lazy" %>

    <p>
      The triangle shows how thoughts, emotions, and behaviors can reinforce
      a painful experience or help you move toward a healthier response.
      Cognitive distortions fit into this because they can lead you to
      interpret a situation inaccurately. When a distorted thought passes
      without question, it can affect how you feel and what you do. Your
      behavior can then reinforce the original thought, creating a cycle
      that becomes difficult to recognize, interrupt, or break.
    </p>

    <p>
      The point is to understand how the pieces connect. You can't always
      control your automatic thoughts or first reactions, but you can learn
      to question your assumptions and choose how to respond. Changing one
      part of the triangle can influence the others. That gives you a chance
      to interrupt a pattern instead of repeating it.
    </p>

    <p>
      Stress can make these patterns harder to notice. A stressor is an
      internal or external event that activates a stress response in the
      body. That response can involve physical and emotional reactions to
      a perceived threat. One way to help complete the stress cycle is to:
    </p>

    <!-- STRESS CYCLE -->
    <ul class="iop-essay-list">
      <li>Move your body.</li>
      <li>Breathe.</li>
      <li>Connect with someone.</li>
      <li>Laugh.</li>
      <li>Give or receive affection.</li>
      <li>Express yourself creatively.</li>
      <li>Cry.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Let's go back to the wild statement I wrote in jail:
      <em>“Hell, nobody will even notice I'm gone.”</em>
      What was really going on?
    </p>

    <p>
      I put on my Sherlock Holmes hat and looked for the distortions with
      my magnifying glass. I found absolute thinking, mind reading, fortune
      telling, emotional reasoning, and overgeneralization. I was treating
      a lack of communication as proof that people didn't care about me.
      Because I wasn't receiving messages or mail at that moment, I decided
      I knew what the silence meant.
    </p>

    <p>
      I had started equating silence with abandonment and projecting my fear
      of being forgotten onto everyone else. I didn't consider other
      explanations: people might have been busy, working, paying bills, or
      doing other normal adult things. If I wasn't being seen or heard, I
      assumed I wasn't on anyone's mind—and good luck getting back there.
    </p>

    <p>
      My brain took that situation and ran with it. Like needy, codependent
      partners, cognitive distortions rarely operate alone. Five of them
      were working together while I was feeling abandoned. They backed each
      other up like brainwashed cultists.
    </p>

    <p>
      The observable fact was limited or no communication. For some reason,
      I thought the world should be contacting poor little me because I was
      locked up. Since my friends weren't contacting me, I decided they had
      forgotten my hardships and forgotten me altogether. The thought
      <em>Everyone has forgotten me</em> sounds like a cognitive distortion
      or two. Overgeneralization and mind reading, with a dash of absolute
      thinking.
    </p>

    <p>
      The whole sentence—<em>Nobody will even notice I'm gone</em>—was a
      pileup of distortions. I was predicting a negative outcome
      (<strong>fortune telling</strong>), assuming that
      <em>nobody</em> would care (<strong>absolute thinking</strong> and
      <strong>mind reading</strong>), and using the behavior of a few people
      to decide what everyone thought (<strong>overgeneralization</strong>).
      I focused on the silence and filtered out other evidence
      (<strong>mental filtering</strong>). I treated one stretch without
      mail or messages as proof that it would continue forever
      (<strong>jumping to conclusions</strong> and
      <strong>catastrophizing</strong>).
    </p>

    <p>
      And all of that started with an automatic thought. I wish they made
      thoughts with a stick shift nowadays.
    </p>

    <p>
      Being incarcerated didn't help. Jail was a stressor, and the limited
      communication was another one. Processing both at once fried me, as it
      would anybody in that situation. I didn't yet know how to spot and
      examine cognitive distortions. I first learned about them in therapy
      in jail.
    </p>

    <p>
      You can question a thought while you're stressed, even if your body
      still needs help settling down. In jail, some ways of completing the
      stress cycle were possible—even laughter and positive social
      interaction. You can breathe anywhere; ask a Buddhist. If you want to
      piss off a cellmate, you can pace.
    </p>

    <p>
      Reading was one way I found relief on the inside. It isn't on the list
      above, but it kept me grounded and helped me literally stay sane. I
      could put in earplugs and, for a little while, go somewhere other than
      jail by opening a book.
    </p>

    <p>
      A cognitive triangle analysis starts with the thought:
      <em>Nobody will notice I'm gone.</em> Then you take a left onto
      Emotions Boulevard, where I felt forgotten, abandoned, and unimportant.
      Turn right onto Behavior Drive, where those feelings started shaping
      what I did.
    </p>

    <p>
      I remember desperately checking my jail texting device, listening for
      my name at nightly mail call, dialing people's numbers, and crying
      when the calls wouldn't connect. I wrote pages and pages in my journal
      about feeling abandoned. Eventually I stopped expecting letters, but
      I kept a little hope that I was wrong—that someone would send me
      something.
    </p>

    <p>
      No one did. Does that justify my cognitive distortions? Not
      necessarily. People texted me. I called people many times. I made
      friends in the dorm. For being in jail, I was in a pretty good place.
      The problem was partly that I wasn't getting what I wanted. That
      wasn't the same as the whole world walking out on me or me being
      entitled to a full-blown pity party.
    </p>

    <p>
      If my brain had been a crime scene investigator, it would've been
      fired for doing such a terrible job with the evidence. Cognitive
      distortions and I will probably be exchanging friendship bracelets
      from summer camp for the rest of my life—we all fall into them
      sometimes. But with the right tools, I can call them out by name,
      pause, examine a thought, and reframe it to better reflect reality.
      That way, I don't get played like a CD.
    </p>

  </div>

</article>